| R1 NAT | MASQUERADE present for 10.1.0.0/24; 5 packets confirmed through it |
| DHCP | 600 s leases, renewals succeeding on schedule |
| ICMP to all "internet" IPs | 0% loss: 172.253.62.100, 66.235.200.145, 216.239.35.4, 152.250.31.93 |
| rp_filter | 0 on R1 eth2/eth3 — asymmetric routing not dropping packets |
| ISPA RFC1918 filter | Blocks RFC1918 sources on eth0; NATted traffic unaffected |
| dswa1/2 inter-office ACL | Only filters 10.1/24→10.3/24; does not touch internet traffic |

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation finds no technical fault. Every subsystem on the path from PC1 to the internet is operating correctly. Traffic control on every device in the forwarding path — R1, ISPA, ISPB, CSW1, CSW2, DSWA1, DSWA2, and ASWA2 — shows only the default fq_codel queue with zero drops and no netem, tbf, or htb rate-limiters. All OSPF adjacencies on all devices have been continuously Full for 10–13 minutes with no log entries indicating flaps. R1's single ISPA-derived default route (203.0.113.1 via eth2, kernel route from DHCP) is stable; the 600-second DHCP lease has been renewing on schedule — the lease file confirms a successful renewal cycle during this investigation, with the new expiry extended well into the future. NAT MASQUERADE rules for 10.1.0.0/24 are in place on both WAN interfaces, confirmed active by counter hits. ICMP from PC1 to every simulated internet address (on both ISPA and ISPB loopbacks) records 0% loss and sub-2 ms RTT. The ISPA RFC1918 ingress filter and the dswa1/dswa2 inter-office ACL are deliberate security policies that do not touch internet-bound traffic. The root cause of the helpdesk report is a false premise: there is no network anomaly. The subjective "slow and intermittent" user experience is not backed by any measurable fault; the team should look at client-side issues (browser, endpoint load) or baseline ISP jitter expectations rather than failing over any uplink or restarting any device.
=== END ===